t them clearly and and truthfully  and authentically and so i wanted to  make sure that my memoir wasn't wasn't  candy-coated and that it was really true  and authentic now you have overcome a  lot and as athletes  it takes time but we come to a point  where we can talk about what ended our  careers yes i think one thing we don't  talk about because of maybe our ego how  we were taught not to deal with being  vulnerable we don't talk about mental  health and depression  you open up about that  how did you overcome that  well it was such a journey it was  literally a three-year odyssey  i was just detached disconnected i just  had all these symptoms as you know but  also the the emotional side like you  said is something that we really don't  talk about much so i really wanted to  give voice to that as well so one i  finally got the right care that i needed  and i got the a procedure called  occipital nerve release where i had  nerves dug out of the back of my neck  that was the source of great headaches  and then i was able to go from there to  getting a whole year of therapy for all  the other things that were wrong and  finally was able to get out of it  when i was throwing a lifeline in my  deepest depression and i was finally  able to get to the point where  i could get help and and make a make a  rise from there you write so movingly in  the book and it's shown in the  documentary too and i'm so glad to see  your gold medals here because that's a  whole other story you're standing on a  bridge you were thinking about i'm going  to jump in i'm going to take my life you  had a traumatic brain injury you had  broken up with a partner you had no  money right you pawned your gold medals  and you said i might as well end it all  but you didn't right why not rihanna  so at the time my mother was coping with  alzheimer's and she and my father were  the most um advent supporters for me all  my life  and the thought of somebody knocking on  her door notifying her that her baby was  gone  um was something i just couldn't bear  and that's what kept me from doing it  yeah and you said you've walked away and  you've never thought about it since  right so glad to hear that thank you and  your gold medals and in the deepest in  your deepest despair you pawned your  gold medals you take a job at a gank a  bank as a bank teller i can't imagine  what would you like 20s 30 i can't  imagine you're doing that that didn't  last very long right but you pawned your  gold medals because you needed money  that badly i did i was in such despair  everything was crumbling around me i  just remember sliding deeper and deeper  into that depression and at the time i  was declared totally disabled  temporarily at the time and so i was  getting in insurance checks from the  insurance company but they would you  know sometimes pay him sometimes not pay  him and string me along and so i had to  get stability yeah and the only thing i  had a value were my medals do you think  you've gotten credit for your career you  know you have that famous game brandi  chastain took off her shirt and one you  know when you won but you saved the kick  i did you saved the kick that allowed  the u.s to win yes i did and all the  focus was on her not dissing brandy at  all of course of course but there was do  you feel hey what about me did you ever  feel that or did you feel  snubbed or neglected people felt that  for you but did you even as you came out  of the game yeah to reflect on your  career right right i appreciate it i i  did i did feel underappreciated that's a  great word um after long time after  because i remember thinking and when  people were saying that to me oh i  didn't see you enough you didn't get the  accolades i remember thinking oh really  i didn't realize that you felt that way  and then i started to think about it and  magnifies it and it magnified it and i  realized it might be more than just my  position as a goalkeeper it might be  that i'm openly 